-- #894 review finding 1: a user's OWN explicit `andThen` call, textually
-- nested inside a `do`-bind's scrutinee (`x <- andThen badBool (...)`), must
-- NOT get the `do`-block ("use `let` instead of `<-`") message — that message
-- is only right for the SYNTHESIZED `andThen` call `lowerDo` builds for the
-- `<-` itself, and this one is neither: there is no `<-` binding `badBool`
-- directly, `andThen` is applied explicitly by the user. A name+ambient-state
-- detector (checking the callee's name plus "are we inside a `do`-lowered
-- chain") false-positives here because it can't tell this call apart from the
-- synthesized one; the fix must key off AST identity instead. Expected: the
-- ORIGINAL container/List/Array message.
badBool : Bool
badBool = True

f = do
  x <- andThen badBool (y => Ok 1)
  Ok x

main = println f
